  "body": "## Finding Description and Impact\n\nSummary\n\nWhen users withdraw kHYPE tokens, the protocol uses the exchange rate from when the withdrawal was queued rather than when it is confirmed to determine how much HYPE token they receive. This allows users to time their withdrawals and profit when the exchange rate decreases between queuing and confirmation, effectively extracting value from the protocol.\n\nWhen a user queues a withdrawal of kHYPE tokens via the queueWithdrawal function, they provide the kHYPE amount they want to withdraw. The exchange rate is then used to determine how much HYPE token they can withdraw.\n\nOnce the minimumum withdrawal period ends(7 days minimum) the user can withdraw the equivalent amount of HYPE tokens at any time by calling the confirmWithdrawal function, which in turn calls the _processConfirmation function.\n\nHowever this amount is based off an outdated exchange rate set during queue because If the exchange rate changes during period between queue and confirmation, it will render the exchange rate calculation in queue irrelevant.\n\nThe vulnerability exists during confirmation of withdrawal, specifically in the _processConfirmation function in the StakingManager contract:\n````\nfunction _processConfirmation(address user, uint256 withdrawalId) internal returns (uint256) {\n    WithdrawalRequest memory request = _withdrawalRequests[user][withdrawalId];\n    \n    // Skip if request doesn't exist or delay period not met\n    if (request.hypeAmount == 0 || block.timestamp < request.timestamp + withdrawalDelay) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    // @audit: Using hype amount based off old exchange rate during queue\n    uint256 hypeAmount = request.hypeAmount;  \n    uint256 kHYPEAmount = request.kHYPEAmount;\n    uint256 kHYPEFee = request.kHYPEFee;\n    \n    // Check kHYPE balances\n    require(kHYPE.balanceOf(address(this)) >= kHYPEAmount + kHYPEFee, \"Insufficient kHYPE balance\");\n    \n    // Update state\n    totalQueuedWithdrawals -= hypeAmount;\n    totalClaimed += hypeAmount;\n    delete _withdrawalRequests[user][withdrawalId];\n    \n    // Burn kHYPE tokens (excluding fee)\n    kHYPE.burn(address(this), kHYPEAmount);\n    \n    // Transfer fee to treasury\n    kHYPE.transfer(treasury, kHYPEFee);\n    \n    emit WithdrawalConfirmed(user, withdrawalId, hypeAmount);\n    \n    return hypeAmount;\n}\n````\nAs we can see above the protocol uses the hype amount calculated during queue, which is at least 7 days old, allowing users to profit if the exchange rate decreases during this period.\n\nConsider the following:\n\n1. User A deposits 100 HYPE and receives 100 kHYPE tokens\n2. The exchange rate improves (e.g., due to rewards): 100 kHYPE = 110 HYPE\n3. User A queues a withdrawal of their 100 kHYPE\n4. System calculates they'll receive 110 HYPE (minus fees)\n5. During the withdrawal delay period, the exchange rate drops due to slashing events. New rate: 100 kHYPE = 90 HYPE.\n6. User A confirms the withdrawal after withdraw delay period.\n7. They receive the originally calculated 110 HYPE.\n8. But their kHYPE was only worth 90 HYPE at confirmation time.\n9. They have profited 20 HYPE from the protocol.\n\nThis vulnerability essentially gives users an incentive to time when they withdraw, to extract excess value from the protocol during downturns, creating a \"bank run\" risk and potentially leading to protocol insolvency.\n\n## Mitigation\n\nUpdate the _processConfirmation as follows:\n\n````\n    /**\n     * @dev Internal function to process a single withdrawal confirmation\n     * @param user Address of the user\n     * @param withdrawalId ID of the withdrawal\n     * @return amount The amount processed, 0 if skipped\n     */\n    function _processConfirmation(address user, uint256 withdrawalId) internal returns (uint256) {\n        WithdrawalRequest memory request = _withdrawalRequests[user][withdrawalId];\n\n        // Skip if request doesn't exist or delay period not met\n        if (request.hypeAmount == 0 || block.timestamp < request.timestamp + withdrawalDelay) {\n            return 0;\n        }\n\n        uint256 hypeAmount = request.hypeAmount;\n        uint256 kHYPEAmount = request.kHYPEAmount;\n        uint256 kHYPEFee = request.kHYPEFee;\n\n        // Check kHYPE balances\n        require(kHYPE.balanceOf(address(this)) >= kHYPEAmount + kHYPEFee, \"Insufficient kHYPE balance\");\n\n        uint256 currentHypeAmount = stakingAccountant.kHYPEToHYPE(kHYPEAmount);\n        // Update state\n        totalQueuedWithdrawals -= request.hypeAmount; // Original amount for accounting consistency\n        totalClaimed += currentHypeAmount; // Actual amount being withdrawn\n        delete _withdrawalRequests[user][withdrawalId];\n\n        // Burn kHYPE tokens (excluding fee)\n        kHYPE.burn(address(this), kHYPEAmount);\n\n        // Transfer fee to treasury\n        kHYPE.transfer(treasury, kHYPEFee);\n\n        emit WithdrawalConfirmed(user, withdrawalId, hypeAmount);\n\n        // Return the current value, not the stored value\n        return currentHypeAmount;\n    }\n````\n\nRun the POC again, it will fail properly with \"FAIL: User should receive more HYPE than current value:\" since user now only receives the current value of HYPE based on current exchange rate.",
  "proof_of_concept": "add the following POC to StakingManager.t.sol:\n\n````\nfunction test_ExchangeRateVulnerabilityDuringWithdrawal() public {\n    address testUser = makeAddr(\"testUser\");\n    uint256 initialStake = 100 ether;\n    \n    // First, set a higher maximum stake amount\n    vm.prank(manager);\n    stakingManager.setMaxStakeAmount(200 ether); // Set max stake to 200 ether\n    \n    // Setup validator and delegation\n    vm.startPrank(manager);\n    validatorManager.activateValidator(validator);\n    validatorManager.setDelegation(address(stakingManager), validator);\n    vm.stopPrank();\n    \n    // Set shorter withdrawal delay for testing\n    vm.prank(manager);\n    stakingManager.setWithdrawalDelay(1 days);\n\n    // 1. Initial state - User deposits when exchange rate is 1:1\n    vm.deal(testUser, initialStake);\n    vm.prank(testUser);\n    stakingManager.stake{value: initialStake}();\n    \n    // Check initial kHYPE balance\n    uint256 initialKHYPEBalance = kHYPE.balanceOf(testUser);\n    console.log(\"Initial kHYPE balance:\", initialKHYPEBalance / 1e18);\n    \n    // 2. Simulate exchange rate improvement (through rewards)\n    // Create and activate a validator for rewards\n    address rewardValidator = makeAddr(\"rewardValidator\");\n    vm.startPrank(admin);\n    validatorManager.grantRole(validatorManager.MANAGER_ROLE(), admin);\n    validatorManager.activateValidator(rewardValidator);\n    vm.stopPrank();\n    \n    // Report rewards to improve exchange rate\n    vm.prank(address(oracleManager));\n    validatorManager.reportRewardEvent(rewardValidator, 10 ether);\n    \n    // Check the new exchange rate through conversion\n    uint256 improvedHYPEValue = stakingAccountant.kHYPEToHYPE(initialKHYPEBalance);\n    console.log(\"kHYPE value after rewards (in HYPE):\", improvedHYPEValue / 1e18);\n    \n    // 3. User queues withdrawal with improved exchange rate\n    vm.startPrank(testUser);\n    kHYPE.approve(address(stakingManager), initialKHYPEBalance);\n    \n    uint256 withdrawalId = stakingManager.nextWithdrawalId(testUser);\n    stakingManager.queueWithdrawal(initialKHYPEBalance);\n    vm.stopPrank();\n    \n    // Get the queued withdrawal details\n    IStakingManager.WithdrawalRequest memory request = stakingManager.withdrawalRequests(testUser, withdrawalId);\n    console.log(\"Queued withdrawal HYPE amount:\", request.hypeAmount / 1e18);\n    console.log(\"Queued withdrawal kHYPE amount:\", request.kHYPEAmount / 1e18);\n    console.log(\"Queued withdrawal kHYPE fee:\", request.kHYPEFee / 1e18);\n    \n    // 4. Simulate exchange rate decrease (through slashing)\n    vm.prank(address(oracleManager));\n    validatorManager.reportSlashingEvent(rewardValidator, 20 ether);\n    \n    // Check the new lower exchange rate\n    uint256 reducedHYPEValue = stakingAccountant.kHYPEToHYPE(initialKHYPEBalance);\n    console.log(\"kHYPE value after slashing (in HYPE):\", reducedHYPEValue / 1e18);\n    \n    // 5. User B stakes 200 ether\n    vm.deal(address(stakingManager), 200 ether);\n    \n    // Advance time past the withdrawal delay\n    vm.warp(block.timestamp + 2 days);\n    \n    // Get user's initial balance before confirmation\n    uint256 userInitialBalance = testUser.balance;\n    \n    // 6. User confirms withdrawal and receives the amount based on OLD exchange rate set on queue\n    vm.prank(testUser);\n    stakingManager.confirmWithdrawal(withdrawalId);\n    \n    uint256 userFinalBalance = testUser.balance;\n    uint256 actualWithdrawn = userFinalBalance - userInitialBalance;\n    \n    console.log(\"User received HYPE:\", actualWithdrawn / 1e18);\n    \n    // 7. User received more than current exchange rate value\n    assertGt(actualWithdrawn, reducedHYPEValue, \"User should receive more HYPE than current value\");\n    console.log(\"Excess HYPE received:\", (actualWithdrawn - reducedHYPEValue) / 1e18);\n}\n````\n\nPOC should pass, effectively showing that user can receive more HYPE token than current exchange rate.",
